{{Community}}
In [[WARFRAME]], [[damage]] is applied to [[health]] in discrete chunks that are represented by individual '''hit''' registrations against a target's [[hitbox]]. When a player presses the "Attack" [[keybinding|keybind]], they may deal one or more damage instances to targets. On successful hit, these damage instances have a chance to roll for their respective damage type's [[Status Effect]] and other "on-hit" effects. [[Damage over Time]] effects can be thought as applying hits to the center of the target's hitbox, but will not roll for Status Effects or trigger "on-hit" effects.

This article explains a commonly used algorithm in [[wikipedia:Shooter game|shooter video games]] and [[wikipedia:Fighting game|fighting games]] for processing attack hits and describes its implementation in WARFRAME from the perspective of a player. It is unknown if the game uses the same algorithm when processing [[enemies]] and [[NPC]] hits against players.

== Overview ==

=== Terminology ===
[[File:English The number of hits from attacking enemies2.png|thumb|right|Example hierarchy of hit data types]]
There are three important hit [[wikipedia:data structure|data structures]]:

* DmgSrc - "Damage Source", the lowest unit of hit registration that can be produced from an attack input

* HitPtr - "Hit [[wikipedia:Pointer (computer programming)|Pointer]]"; a Hit Pointer contains one or more Damage Sources

* HitStruct - "Hit [[wikipedia:Struct|Structure]]"; a Hit Structure contains one or more Hit Pointers
**HitStruct essentially represents the number of hit-related event resolutions per game tick. At most, there can be only 2 HitStructs per game tick. In short, it's the event resolution count per game tick.

=== Damage Source ===
DmgSrc represents each individual hit of an attack. This is visually represented by a single [[Damage Number]] on the screen if the [[Heads-Up Display]] Damage Number is set to "Legacy" mode.

DmgSrc may contain information on:
*[[Damage Type]] distribution
*Whether or not a [[Critical Hit]] has occurred based on [[Critical Chance]]
*If a Critical Hit has occured, the Critical Tier
*Whether or not a [[Status Effect]] will proc based on [[Status Chance]]
*If a Status Effect will proc, the actual Status Effect based on Damage Type distribution
*Whether or not there is a [[Forced Proc]]
*Other "On-Hit" effect rolls

Note that this is a simplification of the DmgSrc data structure; there may be additional logic used to affect the contents of DmgSrc. For example, a {{D|Toxin}} proc will never be able to critically hit so the associated {{D|Toxin}} DoT effect's DmgSrc will likely say that it will not crit.

=== Hit Pointer Structure ===
{{UpdateMe|So are Status HitPtrs OtherPtrs? Probably not since Extra Hits can proc and thus create Status HitPtrs. Status HitPtrs are their own category from MainPtr and OtherPtr}}
{{UpdateMe|Need to elaborate that [[Continuous Weapon]]s with [[Multishot]] do not produce additional damage instances. Instead, Multishot bonuses act as a damage multiplier.}}
A HitPtr is a data structure that contains one or more DmgSrcs.

* HitPtrs are categorized into Main Hit Pointer (MainPtr), Other Hit Pointer (OtherPtr), and Status Hit Pointer (StatusPtr) types. See [[#HitPtr Types]] for more details.
** MainPtrs are generally determined by attacks that require hitbox collision detection, usually those with [[ammo]] consumption or melee attack inputs. See [[Hit Detection]].
** OtherPtrs can only exist when connected to a MainPtr. In other words, OtherPtrs must have one associated MainPtr as its "parent". Thus, OtherPtrs increase the number of HitPtrs based on MainPtrs.
** StatusPtrs come from damage-dealing [[Status Effect]] procs (e.g. {{D|Slash}} or {{D|Blast}}) and can be produced from a MainPtr or OtherPtr.
** The total number of HitPtrs is calculated by adding all the MainPtrs, OtherPtrs, and StatusPtrs.
*** For example, {{Weapon|Kuva Quartakk}} Burst-Fire While Aiming consumes 4 ammo, resulting in 4 HitPtrs; these 4 HitPtrs are all MainPtrs.
*** For example, {{Weapon|Tenet Envoy}}, when a projectile hits an enemy, it generates two MainPtrs: one [[AoE]] hit and one direct projectile hit.
<hr />
* The total damage of a HitPtr is the sum of its DmgSrcs.
** For example, if a weapon has [[Multishot]] of 2.1, and two projectiles are fired, the HitPtr damage is calculated as 2 times the damage at Multishot = 1. If three projectiles are fired, the HitPtr damage is 3 times the damage at Multishot = 1.
** For example, {{Weapon|Cernos Prime}} has a Multishot of 3. Assuming the total damage is 9000, that total damage is the total HitPtr damage. With 3 projectiles, the HitPtr divides that total damage into 3 DmgSrcs, each projectile dealing 3000 damage.
<hr />
Notes:
* Many [[Continuous Weapon]] consume only 0.5 ammo per shot, so consuming 1 ammo will result in two MainPtr structures.
* Melee HitPtrs separate the left-hand and right-hand parts of dual-wielding weapons (e.g. [[Dual Swords]], [[Dual Nikanas]], [[Fist]], [[Tonfa]], etc.)
** For example, {{Weapon|Kronen}} Heavy Attack hitting an enemy will produce two MainPtrs: one for the left-hand Kronen and one for the right-hand Kronen.

=== Hit Structure ===
A HitStruct is a data structure that contains one or more HitPtrs. It represents a segment of [[Health]] reduced from an enemy when a single attack hits the enemy.

*For example, if you shoot an enemy twice with a {{Weapon|Karak}}, the enemy will lose two segments of health corresponding to two HitStructs. If you shoot the enemy only once, the enemy will lose one segment of health corresponding to one HitStruct.

* If multiple DmgSrcs hit the enemy at different times, multiple HitStructs will be created.
* StatusPtrs are all determined based on the number of HitPtrs in a HitStruct.

* MainPtrs that hit the enemy simultaneously will establish their own HitStruct, and there may be more than one HitStruct.
** Because StatusPtrs calculate their count based on HitStructs.

